<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco de Zurbarán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1598">1598</a>–<a href="/years/1664">1664</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco de Zurbarán was a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> painter, active mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. He is best known for austere religious paintings of monks, saints and martyrs, painted for monasteries with strong contrasts of light and shadow, and for a few still lifes of great simplicity. Many of his works were exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, and he spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, overshadowed by Murillo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-zurbaran.jpg" alt="Francisco de Zurbarán" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco de Zurbarán · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_de_Zurbar%C3%A1n_autoportrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
